\title{A Hausdorff Space Need Not Be Compact}
\taxon{Example}
\p{
    Every Euclidean space #{\mathbb{R}^n}, for #{n\geq1}, is Hausdorff. Given
    distinct points #{x,y}, choose #{\varepsilon=d(x,y)/3}. The Euclidean balls
    #{B_\varepsilon(x)} and #{B_\varepsilon(y)} are disjoint by the triangle
    inequality, so they separate the two points.
}
\p{
    Hausdorffness does not force compactness. On the real line, the collection
    ##{
        \{(-n,n)\mid n\in\mathbb{N},\;n\geq1\}
    }
    is an open cover of #{\mathbb{R}}, but every finite subcollection is
    contained in one interval #{(-N,N)} and therefore fails to cover the real
    line.
}
